.TH DUP 2
.SH NAME
dup \- duplicate an open file descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.PP
.B
int dup(int oldfd, int newfd)
.SH DESCRIPTION
Given a file descriptor,
.IR oldfd ,
referring to an open file,
.I dup
returns a new file descriptor referring to the same file.
.PP
If
.I newfd
is \-1 the system chooses the lowest available file descriptor.
Otherwise,
.I dup
will use
.I newfd
for the new file descriptor
(closing any old file associated with
.IR newfd ).
File descriptors are allocated dynamically;
a process has at most 5000 of them, and
.I newfd
must be below that.
(9front-ng: 9front requires
.I newfd
to be no greater than 20 more than the highest file descriptor ever used by
the program; POSIX's
.I dup2
takes any descriptor below
.BR OPEN_MAX ,
which is this limit in APE.)
.PP
.I Dup
does not copy the per file descriptor
.B OCEXEC
flag,
meaning that
.I newfd
will not be closed on
.IR exec (2)
syscall,
when
.I oldfd
had been previously opend with it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libc/9syscall
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR dup (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Sets
